to-patrick-collison-source : List Word
to-patrick-collison-source = lines-of (cons "# Patrick Collison"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Patrick, this is written to you as the person who asked, in public and with numbers, why science now buys less discovery per dollar than it used to, and who then built institutions to test answers. Hyperactivity is an answer from a different place: the medium knowledge is kept in. Everything of yours it builds on is credited to you in full. Each claim below about Avik Jain's work links to the block of his paper, *hyperactivity*, Part I, that states it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Where you started"
  (cons ""
  (cons "In January 2005, at sixteen, you won the Young Scientist and Technology Exhibition with Croma, a language of your own in the Lisp family. Hyperactivity begins in that lineage too: the paper names Lisp reflection among the foundational antecedents of a language that operates on its own descriptions"
  (cons (cite s6-p2 "(§6)")
  (cons ". A routine, the requirement it meets and the reasoning that establishes it are terms of one language"
  (cons (cite s1-p25 "(1.5)")
  (cons ", and definitions are content-addressed objects with their typed syntax and dependency references"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p2 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ". Lisp made code into data you can compute on; here a claim and its evidence are data you can compute on."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## The question you put to science"
  (cons ""
  (cons "In 2018 you and Michael Nielsen argued in The Atlantic that science is getting less return for its investment. In 2019 you and Tyler Cowen argued for Progress Studies, a field to understand what produces progress and how to get more of it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Hyperactivity gives one mechanism for the slowdown. The cost of building on a result has three parts: finding it, trusting it, and fitting it to your own work. In papers, PDFs and codebases all three are paid again by every reader, and they grow with the literature."
  (cons ""
  (cons "In the paper's medium a peer sends a definition with its dependency closure and its proofs, and the recipient checks and uses them in its own context"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p7 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ". A retained result carries a dependency certificate recording what it observed, and matching it is sufficient to reuse a deterministic result"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p6 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ". Where two representations are equivalent, the equivalence gives a path and transport carries what depends on one to the other"
  (cons (cite s1-p27 "(§1)")
  (cons ". A change to a dependency invalidates exactly those retained results whose certificates no longer hold"
  (cons (cite s10-9-p3 "(§10.9)")
  (cons ". That is a medium in which building on a result is a lookup and a check, not a re-derivation."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Fast Grants and Arc, read as experiments"
  (cons ""
  (cons "In April 2020 you and Cowen launched Fast Grants, funding COVID-19 science with decisions in days. In 2021 you co-founded the Arc Institute with Silvana Konermann and Patrick Hsu, with Stanford, UC Berkeley and UCSF as partners and more than $650 million from donors, giving core investigators renewable eight-year appointments. Both treat the institutions of science as something you can redesign and measure."
  (cons ""
  (cons "In February 2025 Arc and its collaborators released Evo 2, a genomic model with 40 billion parameters trained on 9.3 trillion DNA base pairs; in June 2025, State, its first virtual cell model, predicting how gene expression shifts under chemical, genetic or cytokine perturbations. These models produce predictions at a scale no lab can check by hand. What they lack is a place where each prediction sits next to what supports it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "That place is the direction Avik now rates among his most valuable: a chemistry and biology knowledge graph for medicine research. The paper gives the form for what such models output. An observation determines the fiber of histories compatible with it, and what it warrants is a construction over that whole fiber"
  (cons (cite s1-p8 "(1.1)")
  (cons (cite s1-p12 "(1.3)")
  (cons "; refining the observation can only shrink the fiber, so an established conclusion stays established"
  (cons (cite s1-p13 "(§1)")
  (cons ". When a probabilistic description is supplied, its probability structure is additional information on which further reasoning operates"
  (cons (cite s1-p14 "(§1)")
  (cons ". A virtual-cell prediction and a wet-lab result then join one record, each as what it is."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Stripe, and protocols"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Stripe turned payments into a small number of programmable interfaces: protocol engineering at the scale of the economy. At Citadel Securities, Avik and Gašper Ažman built a language for describing arbitrary market-data protocols, which generated optimal C++ parsers built entirely from templates, aimed ultimately at NIC and FPGA code. Gašper's part in that work is credited in full. In the paper the first Futamura projection is an instance of the specialization equation"
  (cons (cite s6-p15 "(§6)")
  (cons ", and the specializer returns, beside the fast code, a code for its justification"
  (cons (cite s6-2-p6 "(§6.2)")
  (cons ". A protocol change is a transformation of the represented source and its dependent constructions, its proof re-established, not a textual edit"
  (cons (cite s10-11-p2 "(§10.11)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Why this is written to you"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Avik considers you one of the people who can take in depth from many fields at once and see what it is for. You built the evidence for the slowdown, the field to study it, and the institutions to test fixes. This is a fix at the level of the medium. Arc is the place it would show its value first, and your own record of funding things fast and measuring the result is the right test for it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Sources"
  (cons ""
  (cons "- Patrick Collison, Wikipedia, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patrick_Collison"
  (cons "- Arc Institute launch, Business Wire, December 2021, https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20211215005308/en/The-Arc-Institute-Launches-to-Accelerate-Scientific-Breakthroughs-in-Complex-Diseases-in-Collaboration-with-Stanford-University-UCSF-and-UC-Berkeley"
  (cons "- Responses to We Need a New Science of Progress, https://patrickcollison.com/progress"
  (cons "- Arc's State virtual cell model, BiopharmaTrend, June 2025, https://biopharmatrend.com/post/1302-arc-institute-releases-its-first-virtual-cell-model-trained-on-270-million-cells"
  (cons "- Stripe Press announced, Marginal Revolution, July 2018, https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2018/07/stripe-press.html"
  (cons "- Avik Jain, hyperactivity, Part I, the blocks linked above"
  nil)))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))
